.TH OMBRA 1 "2026-08-09" "ombra" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ombra \- control and read the ombra thermal daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ombra
.RI [ --socket " " path ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B ombra export
.RI [ --format " " csv|json ]
.RI [ --from " " ts ]
.RI [ --to " " ts ]
.RI [ --ring " " file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The scriptable face of
.BR ombrad (8).
Every command travels over the daemon's control socket except
.BR export ,
which reads the telemetry ring straight off disk: the daemon keeps no history,
the ring is a file, and a week of samples has no business going through a line
protocol.
.PP
The socket is owned by the group
.BR ombra ,
so a member of that group drives the daemon without
.BR sudo .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Reading
.TP
.B status
Current readings and setpoints as JSON. The payload alone is printed, without
the protocol's framing, so it goes straight into a parser.
.TP
.B config show
What the daemon has saved, as JSON: where the settings file is, whether it
exists yet, the mode applied at boot, and every definition.
.TP
.BI "export [--format csv|json] [--from " ts "] [--to " ts "] [--ring " file ]
The recorded samples. Nothing is averaged: the peaks are what the mode
parameters are tuned against. See
.B ombra export --help
for the defaults.
.SS Acting now
.TP
.BI "mode " id
Select a mode. Cancels live tuning and any fan command.
.TP
.BI "set " key = value " ..."
Live tuning, applied at once and saved only when told to. Keys:
.BR pl1 ,
.BR pl2 ,
.BR max_perf_pct ,
.BR turbo ,
.BR epp ,
.BR hwp_boost ,
.BR gt_max_mhz ,
.BR profile ,
.BR fan .
.B profile
picks one of the three fan curves the firmware carries
.RB ( quiet ,
.BR balanced ,
.BR performance );
.B fan
is
.B auto
for that curve or
.B max
for full speed.
.TP
.B reset
Drop live tuning and return to the selected mode.
.TP
.B mark good|too-loud|too-slow
Stamp a judgement onto the current sample, so an opinion made by ear stays
traceable to the numbers that produced it.
.TP
.B fan max|auto
Shorthand for
.BI "set fan=" value .
Full speed is roughly 6100 RPM; nothing reaches it without being told to.
.TP
.BI "charge " percent
The battery charge threshold. Remembered across a reboot, because the node
itself is not.
.SS The backlight
.TP
.B brightness on|off
Whether the backlight follows the ambient light sensor. Remembered across a
reboot; neither switching it on nor off moves the backlight by itself.
.TP
.BI "brightness level " percent
Set the backlight, 0 to 100, exactly as moving the control would: the level
becomes the baseline the automation works around, and the floor binds it.
.TP
.BI "brightness set " key = value " ..."
The backlight settings, applied at once and written only by
.BR "config set brightness" :
.B response_pct
is how far the room may move the backlight either way from that baseline,
.B floor_pct
is the level nothing goes below, and
.B curve
is the
.IR lux : percent
table the room is read through.
.SS The saved definitions
.TP
.BI "config set mode " id " " key = value " ..."
A durable mode definition. Keys:
.BR label ,
.BR platform_profile ,
.BR pl1_w ,
.BR pl2_w ,
.BR max_perf_pct ,
.BR turbo ,
.BR epp ,
.BR hwp_dynamic_boost ,
.BR gt_max_mhz ,
.BR fan .
There are exactly three modes and they are edited in place: the desktop panel
has three slots, and a fourth definition would live where the panel cannot
reach it.
.TP
.BI "config set brightness " key = value " ..."
The backlight settings, written down:
.BR response_pct ,
.BR floor_pct ,
.BR curve .
There are no brightness profiles: the room is one room, and what varies in it
is the light rather than the preference.
.TP
.BI "config save live to " id
Keep the current live tuning as that mode's definition.
.TP
.BI "config default_mode " id
The mode applied at boot.
.TP
.B config reset modes|brightness|all
Back to the values compiled into the daemon. This is the way out of a mess and
the state of a fresh install, not a routine operation.
.TP
.B reload
Re-read the settings file. Not normally needed: a file changed from outside is
picked up on its own.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " path"
The control socket. Default
.IR /run/ombra/control.sock .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The daemon accepted the command.
.TP
.B 1
The daemon refused it, or could not be reached.
.TP
.B 2
The command line itself was wrong.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/ombra/control.sock
The control socket, owned by the group
.BR ombra .
.TP
.I /var/lib/ombra/settings.conf
The saved definitions. Written by the daemon; it appears at the first change.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ombra/state
What the daemon remembers across a reboot: the selected mode, whether the
backlight follows the room, and the charge threshold.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ombra/metrics.ring
The telemetry ring. A ring written in an older layout is converted when the
daemon next opens it; one it cannot read is kept as
.I metrics.ring.bak
and replaced by a fresh ring.
.SH EXAMPLES
Tune by ear, then keep what was found:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ombra set pl1=15 max_perf_pct=40
ombra mark good
ombra config save live to quiet
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read an hour of samples into a spreadsheet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ombra export --format csv --from $(date -d '1 hour ago' +%s) > session.csv
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ombrad (8),
.BR ombra-tray (1)
